1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,240
You drove the scheduler. Now open the hood. You only read these files; you don't

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,540
edit them. Nothing here needs to be memorised.

3
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:18,368
In scheduler .h, line 20 says there are three task slots. Line 22 names the shape of

4
00:00:18,368 --> 00:00:19,020
a task.

5
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:26,000
Lines 33 to 37 are each task's card. Line 35, the stack pointer, must come first,

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,840
because the switching code reads the first word.

7
00:00:30,340 --> 00:00:37,087
Lines 41 and 44 share the current task and the tick count. Lines 50 and 57 are the

8
00:00:37,087 --> 00:00:38,980
two functions you used.

9
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:48,345
Now scheduler.c. Line 11 gives each task 256 words of stack. Line 12 sets the bit

10
00:00:48,345 --> 00:00:51,340
that says Thumb instructions.

11
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:57,472
Lines 14 to 16 are the stacks, the cards, and the current position. Lines 18 and 19

12
00:00:57,472 --> 00:00:59,440
are shared with the switcher.

13
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:06,040
Lines 25 to 33 catch a task that returns by mistake. It waits here forever.

14
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:14,160
Lines 43 to 56 build a pretend saved state on a new task's stack.

15
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:17,700
Line 46 writes the status value.

16
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,280
Line 48 writes your function's address, so the first turn starts there.

17
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:25,660
Line 50 points the return at the safety net.

18
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:31,440
Lines 51 to 54 leave room for the other registers.

19
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:34,980
Line 55 returns the new stack position.

20
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:42,842
Line 60 finds the top of this slot's stack. Line 61 forges the bookmark. Line 62

21
00:01:42,842 --> 00:01:44,300
records the name.

22
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:51,322
Line 73 adds one, then takes the remainder after dividing by three. Line 74 makes

23
00:01:51,322 --> 00:01:52,660
that task current.

24
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:55,880
Starting at zero, what are the next three?

25
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:01,380
Your turn. Pause the video.

26
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:05,380
One, two, zero.

27
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:11,260
Line 79 counts a millisecond. Line 82 requests a switch.

28
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:16,240
Lines 90 and 91 give both interrupts the lowest priority.

29
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:19,700
Lines 93 and 94 choose task zero.

30
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:26,120
Line 98 sets the one millisecond tick. Line 100 launches the first task.

31
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:31,600
Now the assembly file. Line 28 reads the old task's stack position.

32
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,580
Lines 30 and 31 find its card.

33
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:40,520
Line 33 makes 32 bytes of room. Line 34 saves the position on the card.

34
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:48,023
Line 36 stores four registers. Lines 37 to 40 move four more down, and line 41

35
00:02:48,023 --> 00:02:49,100
stores them.

36
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:55,140
Lines 43 to 47 pause interrupts and call the chooser.

37
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:59,880
Lines 49 and 50 read the new task's stack position.

38
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:04,400
Lines 52 to 57 reload the upper registers.

39
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:07,220
Line 59 sets the new stack.

40
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:12,840
Lines 61 and 62 reload the other four.

41
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:19,058
Line 64 returns, and the chip restores the rest. The new task wakes where it

42
00:03:19,058 --> 00:03:19,660
stopped.

43
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:24,900
Lines 80 to 84 find task zero's pretend stack.

44
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:28,840
Lines 86 to 88 switch to the task stack.

45
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:36,700
Lines 90 to 93 unpack the bookmark. Lines 95 and 96 allow interrupts and jump in.

46
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:43,614
Draw one task's stack and mark where the saved pointer lands. If you can explain it

47
00:03:43,614 --> 00:03:46,820
to a friend, you understand a real scheduler.

